{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And Moses died there.</b> At the time of his death, Moses said to the Holy One, blessed be He: ״לא אמות כי אחיה״. He said to him: You cannot, for this is the way of all flesh. He said to Him: One thing I ask of You — let all the gates and abysses of the earth split open, and let all see that there is none besides You, as it is said: ״וידעת היום והשבות אל לבבך כי ה׳ הוא האלהים״. Immediately Moses opened and said: ״אין עוד״. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: You said ״אין עוד״ — I too say concerning you: ״ולא קם נביא עוד בישראל כמשה״. Immediately the Holy One, blessed be He, descended in His glory, and three angels with Him — Michael, Gabriel, and Zagzael. Gabriel arranged his bed; Michael spread purple over it; Zagzael placed the covering of circumcision at his head. At that moment the Holy One, blessed be He, stood at his head, and Zagzael at his feet, Michael on this side and Gabriel on that side. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: Join your feet, this one to that one — and so he did. He said to him: Close the eyelids of your eyes, this one over that one — and so he did. Immediately the Holy One, blessed be He, called to the soul of Moses and said to her: My daughter, one hundred and twenty years I decreed for you to be in the body of Moses; depart and do not delay, for your time to depart has arrived. She answered the Holy One, blessed be He: I know that You are the Elohim of spirits and souls, and in Your hand is the soul of all living, and that You created me and placed me in the body of Moses. Is there any body purer into which You could place me more than Moses — in whom there was never maggot or worm, and flies were never seen upon him, and he was never narrow-eyed? I do not wish to depart from him. He said to her again: Do not delay, for your time to depart has arrived, and I will raise you to the highest heavens and seat you among the cherubim beneath the throne of My glory, beside seraphim and ofanim and angels. She said to Him: Master of the worlds, it is better for me to be in the body of Moses than among cherubim and angels — for from among them two descended, Uzza and Azazel, and lusted after the daughters of the earth and came upon them, and they also corrupted their ways until You blotted them out; but Moses — from the time You revealed Yourself to him in the bush, he did not return to his wife. I beg of You, leave me in my place. When He saw this, He kissed him and took his soul with a kiss, as it is said: ״על פי ה׳״. At that moment the Holy One, blessed be He, wept over him and said: Who will rise up for Me against the wicked? Who will pray before Me on behalf of the children? And the ministering angels weep and say: ״והחכמה מאין תמצא״. And ״ונעלמה מעיני כל חי״. And the heavens weep and say: ״אבד חסיד מן הארץ״. And the earth weeps and says: ״וישר באדם אין״. And all the orders of creation weep and say: ״הצדיק אבד ואין איש שם על לב״. And the sun and moon weep and say: ״בינו בוערים בעם״. And all the constellations weep and say: ״וכסילים מתי תשכילו״. And the holy spirit weeps and says: ״ולא קם נביא עוד בישראל כמשה״. And Joshua searched for Moses and did not find him; he wept and said: ״הושיעה ה׳ כי גמר חסיד כי פסו אמונים מבני אדם״. And all Israel weep and say: ״ויתא ראשי עם צדקת ה׳ עשה ומשפטיו עם ישראל״. And the Sages said: these eight verses — a single individual reads them. This is difficult, for regarding all the sections in the Torah as well, we say in tractate Megillah: one reads and not two. And the reason we now have the practice that two read — the reader and the sexton — we rely on what we learned in tractate Bikkurim at the beginning: whoever knew how to read would read, and whoever did not know how to read would be prompted; when they were embarrassed, they enacted that all should be prompted. And so too with these eight verses, the prayer leader prompts along with everyone."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {0 "זגנא\"ל is transliterated as Zagzael, reflecting the unvocalized consonants as written. The eight closing verses of the Torah (Deut 34:5–12) are the subject of the halakhic discussion at the end."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:02:21.180611265Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}